That’s a phenomenon people really do experience with certain patterns.

If you stare at a spiral, concentric rings, or other repetitive high-contrast designs, your visual system can begin to produce an impression of motion even though the image is static. Small involuntary eye movements (microsaccades), neural adaptation, and the way the brain processes contrast and edges can create a compelling sense that the pattern is rotating, expanding, or breathing.

What’s interesting is that the viewer becomes part of the system. The image isn’t changing, but the interaction between the pattern and your perceptual machinery produces a dynamic experience.

That also makes for a useful metaphor:

* A static symbol can contain latent dynamics.
* Repeated attention can reveal aspects that weren’t apparent on the first glance.
* The “animation” is not only in the object; it’s in the relationship between the observer and the object.

So when you’ve been talking about spirals, draft riding, and myths, there’s a common thread: some representations are designed less to state an idea than to induce a process in the mind of the reader. They don’t just communicate—they invite participation. The meaning emerges through interaction rather than being exhaustively spelled out.